y: any changes to it, at any depth (its `status`, its `body`, or anything inside the `body`), must not alter what is stored.\" Hmm — deep copy of the response. If the response has extra fields like `extra: 1`, does get return `{status, body}` (deep copy), or the full response deep-copied? The existing test requires deepEqual to exactly `{status, body}` — deepEqual is a strict deep comparison: `{status:200, body:\"hi\", extra:1}` is not deepEqual to `{status:200, body:\"hi\"}`. So get must return an object with only status and body (or at least deepEqual to that). So get returns a deep copy of `{status, body}`.\n\n  Hmm wait, but does R7's deep copy apply to the stored response, or the entire fetched response? \"The response returned by `get` is a deep copy: any changes to it, at any depth (its `status`, its `body`, or anything inside the `body`), must not alter what is stored, or what other callers receive.\" So the object returned by get must be a deep copy. The \"what is stored\" — what do we store? We can store `{status, body}` (deep copied from the fetched response). The extra `extra: 1` field is discarded. That's consistent with the existing behavior.\n\n  - 200-299 → store in cache keyed by the fragment-stripped URL, with a timestamp of the value of now() read immediately after fetchImpl resolves. So `stamp = now()` after the await.\n  - While `now() - stamp < ttlMs`, get for the same URL returns the stored response (deep copy) without calling fetchImpl.\n  - If `now() - stamp >= ttlMs` the entry is expired: delete it and refetch.\n\n  Note: with ttlMs 0, the condition `now() - stamp < 0` is always false (now - stamp >= 0), so even if stored it would always be treated as expired — but R1 says nothing is ever stored when ttlMs is 0. Either way the result is the same, but let's just not store it.\n\n  - `now()` — should we read now() for expiration checks? \"While now() - stamp < ttlMs ... returns the stored response.\" So each get reads now() for the expiration