e spec — the spec doesn't forbid it.\n\n  Does a fresh call participate in the in-flight map? \"Never wait for an in-progress fetch: it always calls fetchImpl.\" Should the fresh fetch itself be recorded in the in-flight map so that a concurrent non-fresh get joins it? Hmm. R4 says \"if a fetch for a URL is in progress, other get calls for the same URL wait for that fetch.\" A fresh call is a fetch for that URL in progress. But R5 says fresh \"never reads the cache and never waits for an in-progress fetch\" — that's about the fresh call itself. Whether other calls should join the fresh fetch... R4 says \"if a fetch for a URL is in progress\" — any fetch. I think it's cleaner to record all fetches (including fresh) in the in-flight map, so that concurrent normal gets join the in-progress fresh fetch. But wait — that could produce weird behavior: fresh replaces the stored entry, and the joined caller receives the fresh fetch's result. That's consistent with R4.\n\n  Hmm, but let me think about which is more defensible. R5: \"never reads the cache, never waits for an in-progress fetch: always calls fetchImpl.\" Nothing about whether it publishes itself. R4: \"if a fetch for a URL is in progress, other get calls for the same URL (fragment-ignored) wait for that fetch and receive its result (or its rejection) instead of starting a new one. This applies regardless of ttlMs.\"\n\n  I think recording the fresh fetch in the in-flight map is the more literal interpretation of R4 (\"a fetch for a URL is in progress\" — the fresh call is also a fetch for that URL). Let's do that: all fetches (fresh or not) are registered in the in-flight map before calling fetchImpl, and removed on settlement. Non-fresh gets check in-flight first (after cache check? before?).\n\n  Order for non-fresh get:\n  1. Validate URL (TypeError).\n  2. key = strip fragment.\n  3. If ttlMs > 0, check cache: entry exists and is not expired → hit: stats.hits++, return deep copy. Expired → delete entry, cont